<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rukn al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 976</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Hasan ibn Buya · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rukn al-Dawla, born Hasan ibn Buya, was one of the three Buyid brothers who founded the dynasty's rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. He took Ray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> and governed the central and northern Iranian lands until his death in 976, receiving his honorific, 'pillar of the state', from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 946. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> was the statesman Ibn al-Amid, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> later united the Buyid realm.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rukn-al-dawla.jpg" alt="Rukn al-Dawla" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 949-977 work, CNG photograph · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buwayhids_(Buyids)._Rukn_al-Dawla_Abu_%27Ali_al-Hasan._AH_338-366_(949-977)._AR_Portrait_Dirham_(33mm,_5.69_g,_12h)._Al-Muhammadiya_mint._Dated_AH_351_(AD_962-3)_Obverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hasan was the son of Buya, a Dailamite fisherman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a> who left his Zoroastrian faith and converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. His older brother was 'Ali and his younger brother Ahmad, and he also had a sister named Kama. In around 928 'Ali entered the service of Makan, the Samanid governor of Ray, and found military posts for his brothers; Hasan was then about thirty years old. When Makan was defeated by the Ziyarid prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a>, the brothers transferred their allegiance to him.</p><p>Hasan fought in the battles over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, where 'Ali built a realm of his own, and was sent to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardavij">Mardavij</a> as a hostage in around 934. The death of Mardavij in 935 let him escape and opened central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a>. He took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, lost it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziyarid-dynasty">Ziyarids</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vushmgir">Vushmgir</a> three years later, and in 940 appointed Abu 'l-Fadl ibn al-'Amid as his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Hasan recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 940 and occupied Ray in 943. Driven from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> by Ibn Muhtaj, the governor of Samanid Khurasan, he returned to Ray only in 946 or 947, then took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vushmgir">Vushmgir</a>. Control of those provinces changed hands several times until 955, and a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> bound him to respect Ziyarid independence. A less humiliating treaty followed in 971 or 972, though he continued to pay tribute.</p><p>When 'Ali, known as '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a>, died in September 949, Rukn al-Dawla claimed the title of senior amir, and the centre of Buyid power shifted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> to Ray. His armies twice took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, in 949 and again in 967, when the Sallarid Ibrahim I ibn Marzuban I, his son-in-law, was restored there. In 974 he made his son '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> reinstate his cousin '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izz-al-dawla">Izz al-Dawla</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, holding that the line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla">Mu'izz al-Dawla</a> could not be removed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At a meeting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in January 976 he agreed that '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adud-al-dawla">Adud al-Dawla</a> would succeed him as senior amir, while Ray went to Fakhr al-Dawla and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muayyad-al-dawla">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla</a>. He died a few months later. Because he had won central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> almost without his elder brother's help, his descendants and those of '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-al-dawla">Imad al-Dawla</a> each claimed the senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>, which later divided the state. Like the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buyid-dynasty">Buyids</a> he was a Shi'ite, but he protected his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> subjects to prevent internal discord.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rukn al-Dawla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliph al-Mustakfi gave the brothers the honorific titles by which they are known, Imad al-Dawla, <b>Rukn al-Dawla</b> and Mu'izz al-Dawla, 'support', 'pillar' and 'strengthener' of the state; a few weeks later Mu'izz al-Dawla had him blinded and deposed and installed al-Muti in his place.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 948 he was captured by the Buyid <b>Rukn al-Dawla</b>, and his family's hold on Azerbaijan weakened in later succession quarrels.</blockquote><a href="/state/sallarid-dynasty">Sallarid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahijan">Lahijan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Ray, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.21,50&amp;year=966" aria-label="Lahijan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5802 824 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rukn al-Dawla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6252 1094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6252 1094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.21,50&amp;year=966">What was here in 966 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
